# snapshot.epd — the corpus of bench/snapshot.mjs: 60 positions that the golden record (bench/golden.json) hashes.
# A line: the FEN; the move explainMove is asked about (SAN, or - when the position is over); the record id; an optional note.
# How it was chosen: six groups that together reach most of the explanation code, each a fixed share of the 60.
#   open  (10)  the first moves of mainline systems (e4, d4, Ruy Lopez, Najdorf, QGD, Nimzo, KID, Caro-Kann, English), where the opening name speaks.
#   tact  (15)  Win at Chess positions taken evenly through bench/suites/wac.epd (about every 18th position that has a single best move), asked with the suite's move.
#   quiet (10)  maneuvering positions 8 to 12 moves into mainline systems, with no tactic on the board, so the positional terms and plans speak.
#   end   (10)  nine tablebase-sized endgames of 3 to 5 pieces, won, drawn and tricky (KQK, KRK, KPK, KBNK, KQKR, KRKB, Lucena, wrong bishop, KNNK), and one
#               pawn ending of 8 pieces (end-04) that no table covers, so the search-side endgame terms (passed and protected pawns, king activity) speak.
#   mate, sac, blun (10)  moves that explainMove meets: a missed mate in 1 (mate-01), the mate in 1 itself (Scholar's, smothered), a mate in 2 asked by its quiet
#               key move (mate-02), sound and unsound sacrifices, and two real blunders (blun-02 allows a mate in 1, blun-03 loses the queen).
#   term  (5)   positions that are over: two checkmates, a stalemate, the fifty-move rule, insufficient material.
# The opening, quiet and sacrifice positions were made by playing SAN lines of well-known games through the engine's own Game, so every FEN is legal.
# Changing a line changes its hashes: edit this file only together with 'npm run snapshot:update'.

